a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. education;
d. manpower;
e. women's empowerment and child protection;
f. youth and sports;
g. population and civil registration;
h. mental and spiritual; and
i. peace and order.
2. Prevention of acts of violence by OPD and UKPD as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in an integrated and continuous manner based on the Regional Action Plan.
3. In addition to the Regional Government, prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) must be implemented by:
a. family and/or closest relatives;
b. society;
c. educational institutions;
d. health institutions;
e. social community institutions; and
f. business world and other institutions.

CHAPTER VIII

SPECIAL PROTECTION OF CHILDREN

Article 15

1. Special protection of children is provided to:
a. children in emergency situations;
b. children in conflict with the law;
c. children from minority and isolated groups;
d. children who are exploited economically and/or sexually;
e. children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substance abuse;
f. children who are victims of pornography;
g. children with HIV/AIDS;
h. children….
i. children who are victims of kidnapping, sale, and/or trafficking;
j. children who are victims of physical and/or psychological violence;
k. children who are victims of sexual crimes;
l. children who are victims of terrorist networks;
m. children with disabilities;
n. children who are victims of maltreatment and neglect;
o. children with deviant social behavior; and
p. children who are victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their parents' condition.
2. The Regional Government in implementing the handling of special protection of children as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 16

The provision of services to victims is implemented with the principles of:
a. fast;
b. safe and comfortable;
c. sense of empathy;
d. non-discrimination;
e. easily accessible;
f. free of charge; and
g. guaranteed confidentiality.

Article 17

1. Forms of services provided to women and children victims of violence are as follows:
a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. legal….
legal assistance;
d. social rehabilitation and reintegration;
e. repatriation;
f. identification services; and
g. psychological services.
2. Services as referred to in paragraph (1) must be supported by facilities in the form of:
a. special service rooms within the police force;
b. expert and professional personnel;
c. service centers and safe houses; and/or
d. other facilities and infrastructure necessary for victim recovery.
3. Forms of services as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in accordance with minimum service standards established by the government and implemented by related Regional Apparatus Organizations whose duties and functions are in the fields of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. women's empowerment and child protection; and
d. mental and spiritual.
4. In carrying out the duties and functions as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government cooperates with government agencies, other regional governments, non-governmental organizations, and social institutions.
5. Further provisions regarding the procedures for handling services for women and children from violence as referred to in paragraph (1), paragraph (2) and paragraph (3), shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 23

In addition to establishing PPT for the Protection of Women and Children as referred to in Article 22, in order to support the implementation of protection for women and children from violence, the following shall be established:
a. Task force on trafficking in persons;
b. Regional action committee for the elimination of the worst forms of child labor;
c. Task force for the protection of women and children at the sub-district level;
d. Domestic violence prevention groups at the urban village/village level; and
e. Other task forms that support the Integrated Service Center for the Protection of Women and Children.

Article 24

1. The task force as referred to in Article 23 letter a, shall consist of members, among others:
a. representatives from the Regional Government;
b. law enforcement;
c. community organizations;
d. non-governmental organizations;
e. professional organizations; and
f. researchers/academics.
2. The task force as referred to in paragraph (1), is a coordinative institution tasked with coordinating the prevention and handling of trafficking in persons.
3. Further provisions regarding the establishment, duties and functions of the task force as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be stipulated by Regent Decree.

Article 25

1. The Regional Action Committee as referred to in Article 23 letter b, shall consist of members, among others:
a. representatives from the regional government;
b. law enforcement;
c. community….
community organizations;
d. non-governmental organizations;
e. professional organizations; and
f. researchers/academics.
2. Further provisions regarding the duties and functions of the committee as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be stipulated by Regent Decree.

Article 28

1. In order to achieve the objectives of protecting women and children from acts of violence as referred to in Article 3, the Regional Government cooperates with:
a. the Government;
b. the Provincial Government;
c. other Regency/City Governments; and
d. non-governmental institutions.
2. The cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1), includes:
a. provision of data and information;
b. rehabilitation of victims of acts of violence;
c. repatriation and social reintegration; and
d. provision of evidence and witnesses;
3. The cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) shall be implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

CHAPTER XIV

FINANCING

Article 34

The financing of the provision of protection for the rights of women and children from acts of violence is sourced from:

a. the State Revenue and Expenditure Budget;
b. the Provincial Regional Revenue and Expenditure Budget;
c. the Regional Revenue and Expenditure Budget (APBD);
d. the Village Revenue and Expenditure Budget; and
e. Other lawful and non-binding financial sources in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
